formation to the Income feature to reduce its skewness.\n# Usefulness: This transformation helps normalize the highly skewed Income feature, allowing the model to better capture meaningful trends and relationships with the target variable.\ndf['Log_Income'] = df['Income'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))  # Add 1 to avoid log(0).\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Drop Irrelevant or Redundant Columns\nCertain columns may be dropped if they are redundant after feature engineering or provide no additional useful information.\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\n# The DriversLicense feature doesn't significantly contribute to the prediction of credit card approval success in most credit-scoring datasets, \n# as possession of a driver's license has no direct relationship with financial behavior.\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\nBy combining these engineering steps (feature manipulation, interaction terms, and transformation), we enhance the dataset's ability to capture hidden relationships and improve the downstream model’s performance at predicting credit card approval success.", '```python\n# Feature name: PriorDefault_Reversed\n# Feature description: This feature is created by reversing the PriorDefault variable (1 becomes 0 and 0 becomes 1). It represents "no prior defaults" as 1 and "has prior defaults" as 0. This reversal might add value in models where having a positive (1) correlation with success is beneficial for learning.\n# Usefulness: The original `PriorDefault` feature implies greater credit risk (1 for prior defaults and 0 for none). By creating the reversed feature, it represents no prior defaults as 1, aligning positively with success, potentially improving the separability of success versus non-success in credit card applications.\n\ndf[\'PriorDefault_Reversed\'] = 1 - df[\'PriorDefault\']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: DebtIncomeRatio_Log\n# Feature description: This feature is the logarithmic transformation of the `DebtIncomeRatio`.\n# Usefulness: The `DebtIncomeRatio` feature has a highly skewed distribution (extremely high values and a large range). Applying a logarithmic transformation can reduce this skew, making the feature more stable and improving its utility in predictive models.\n\ndf[\'DebtIncomeRatio_Log\'] = df[\'DebtIncomeRatio\'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: Debt_Per_Income_Bin\n# Feature description: This feature bins the `Debt/Income` ratio into categories (e.g., low, medium, high, very high). \n# Usefulness: Similar to the usefulness of binning in risk assessment (low vs high risk), binning this ratio makes the feature more interpretable for the model. Additionally, extreme ratios may be easier to categorize (e.g., "very high" category) and could be strong predictors of unsuccessful applications.\n\nbins = [-np.inf, 0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 10, np.inf]\nlabels = [\'Very Low\', \'Low\', \'Medium\', \'High\', \'Very High\']\ndf[\'Debt_Per_Income_Bin\'] = pd.cut(df[\'DebtIncomeRatio\'], bins=bins, labels=labels)\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[\'Debt_Per_Income_Bin\'], drop_first=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\n# The `DriversLicense` variable shows low variance, with the mean (0.4734) close to the median (0). It is unlikely to provide much information gain for predicting credit card application success. Additionally, the presence of a driver\'s license may not be directly relevant to the credit risk or application success likelihood.\n\ndf.drop(columns=[\'DriversLicense\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Income is dropped\n# The feature `Income` has a highly skewed distribution with extreme outliers. Its minimum value is 0 (indicating zero income) and has a very high standard deviation compared to its mean. This means it is unlikely to add stable predictive power, especially since the `DebtIncomeRatio` is derived from both `Debt` and `Income`, already capturing its impact more effectively.\n\ndf.drop(columns=[\'Income\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why PriorDefault is dropped\n# After creating the `PriorDefault_Reversed` feature, the information in `PriorDefault` is redundant. The new feature aligns with a more intuitive interpretation for modeling success in credit card applications. Hence, we drop the original column to avoid multicollinearity issues.\n\ndf.drop(columns=[\'PriorDefault\'], inplace=True)\n```end']
